<div class="bb-quote"><b>Julia wrote:</b><br /><blockquote class="bb-quote-body">with a pair of shoes you are comfortable in and some practice, you can run as fast in heels as in sneakers.
&nbsp;
</blockquote></div>
Why aren&#39;t Olympic athletes wearing them then?I don&#39;t doubt that some people can run short distances in heel relatively well, but no-one runs &quot;real&quot; non-novelty races in heels, so the &quot;just as fast&quot; bit doesn&#39;t make any sense.

[/quote]

Hey, I was not claiming that spikey heels are reasonable footwear for running. I only said, it can be done with practice, and it can be done in a way that you are not actually any slower then you&#39;d be normally. I know for a fact that I can hit my top speed in boots with a 3 inch block heel, because I have done that (LARPs make you do crazy stuff). The reason they are not worn in the Olympics are probably A: They would in all likelyhood not be legal footwear B: If you fell, you&#39;d fall much worse and C: They will have negative effects on your body in the long run (which really flat shoes do too).

But let me assure you that my personal superhero costume does not involve heels, bathing suits, strapless tops or random areas of exposed skin.&nbsp;
